															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ocuin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6104981); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6104981&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608567377" alt="OCuin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6104981)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ocuin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6104981); return false;" >OCuin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1608565292 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Dec 2020 15:41</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4850252">
									

<p>Summoned by PM</p>
<p>The premise of sirens as weird ads is alright - it's an interesting enough play on the ideas when mixed together, I guess. Unfortunately, the intrigue this builds up is immediately undercut by just having them be monsters that will kill you. Whilst expected given the premise is essentially sirens, it's still just far too standard - murder monsters just aren't very interesting any more, and the vast majority of the site audience will need a lot of convincing to enjoy that kind of article.</p>
<p>The idea of sirens adapting to the modern world is fun, and it's a decent premise for a narrative I think, but you don't do enough with it to really make it work. There's no actual story here, no real series of events, only a premise that might start a narrative. You need to fill out the rest of what's going to happen - tell us the middle and the end of the story too. What's the progression and the arc here? That's the key information we need to know to be confident in the story.</p>
<p>I think you need to put a bit of thought into how you're going to get the reader involved in the story here - whilst the premise is intriguing, it's lacking something to really latch onto because it carries neither mystery nor character really. I'd recommend trying to involve more character into the story so that you can really get the reader involved and caring about your story. The premise <em>might</em> do that, but without something more substantive, I wouldn't be confident it could carry a reader the whole way through the story.</p>
<p>Good luck!</p>

